==English==
[[File:The_Painter_and_His_Pug_by_William_Hogarth.jpg|thumb|{{w|William Hogarth}}, ''The Painter and his Pug'' (1745), with  “The LINE of BEAUTY” bottom-left.]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun|lines of beauty}}

# {{lb|en|fine arts}} An [[curved]] line that is supposedly inherently beautiful. It is represented in different ways by different authors, although usually as a kind of elongated S (like the one in the picture above drawn by {{w|William Hogarth|Hogarth}}).
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Blake|title=Life of William Blake|year=1880|volume=2|chapter=[[s:en:Life of William Blake (1880), Volume 2/Prose writings/Public Address|Public Address and Memoranda by Blake of his mode of Engraving]]|passage=Every line is the '''line of beauty'''; it is only fumble and bungle which cannot draw a line. This only is ugliness. That is not a line which doubts and hesitates in the midst of its course.}}

